{
 "lead": "Legendre's equation is a second-order linear ODE with variable coefficients: (1 − x²)y″ − 2xy′ + n(n + 1)y = 0. For n = 0, 1, 2, …, one solution is a polynomial P<sub>n</sub>(x). These polynomials are orthogonal on [−1, 1], so they expand functions the way sine and cosine do in Fourier series. They appear in physics and engineering: gravitational potential, electrostatics, heat flow in a sphere, and antenna patterns. The singular points x = ±1 explain why the series method needs care at the ends of the interval.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#ordinary-points-and-regular-singular-points\">3.1 point classification</a>, <a href=\"#power-series-solutions-about-an-ordinary-point\">3.2 power series about an ordinary point</a>, and <a href=\"#frobenius-method\">3.3 Frobenius method</a> for behaviour near x = ±1.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "The equation and its singular points",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Legendre's equation is a second-order linear ODE with variable coefficients. The parameter n controls which polynomial solution appears."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "(1 − x²)y″ − 2xy′ + n(n + 1)y = 0",
     "where": "y = y(x); y′ = dy/dx; y″ = d²y/dx²; n is a constant. Polynomial solutions occur when n = 0, 1, 2, … ."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In standard form, y″ − (2x/(1−x²))y′ + (n(n+1)/(1−x²))y = 0. At x = 0 the coefficients are analytic, so x = 0 is an ordinary point. At x = ±1 the denominator 1−x² is zero. After multiplying by 1−x², the coefficient of y″ has a simple zero, so x = ±1 are regular singular points (see <a href=\"#ordinary-points-and-regular-singular-points\">3.1</a>)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The point x = 0 is the centre of the interval. The points x = ±1 are the ends where the coefficient of y″ vanishes."
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "At x = 0, 1−x² ≠ 0, so both coefficient functions in standard form are analytic there.",
      "At x = ±1, 1−x² = 0, so a Frobenius series about an end needs the method of <a href=\"#frobenius-method\">3.3</a>."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Rodrigues' formula and the first four polynomials",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Rodrigues' formula builds each polynomial by differentiating (x²−1)<sup>n</sup> n times."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "P<sub>n</sub>(x) = (1/(2<sup>n</sup> n!)) d<sup>n</sup>/dx<sup>n</sup> [(x² − 1)<sup>n</sup>]",
     "where": "n! = 1·2·…·n; 2<sup>n</sup> = 2 multiplied n times; d<sup>n</sup>/dx<sup>n</sup> is the n-th derivative."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "n = 0: P<sub>0</sub>(x) = 1/(2<sup>0</sup>·0!) d<sup>0</sup>/dx<sup>0</sup>[(x²−1)<sup>0</sup>] = 1.",
      "n = 1: P<sub>1</sub>(x) = 1/(2·1!) d/dx[(x²−1)] = (1/2)(2x) = x.",
      "n = 2: (x²−1)² = x⁴ − 2x² + 1; second derivative = 12x² − 4. P<sub>2</sub>(x) = (12x² − 4)/(2²·2!) = (12x² − 4)/8 = (3x² − 1)/2.",
      "n = 3: (x²−1)³ = x⁶ − 3x⁴ + 3x² − 1; third derivative = 120x³ − 72x = 24(5x³ − 3x). P<sub>3</sub>(x) = 24(5x³ − 3x)/(2³·3!) = (5x³ − 3x)/2."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "(n + 1)P<sub>n+1</sub>(x) = (2n + 1)xP<sub>n</sub>(x) − nP<sub>n−1</sub>(x)",
     "where": "n = 1, 2, 3, …; it gives the next polynomial from the two previous ones."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Parity is fixed by n: P<sub>n</sub>(−x) = (−1)<sup>n</sup>P<sub>n</sub>(x). So P<sub>0</sub> and P<sub>2</sub> are even, and P<sub>1</sub> and P<sub>3</sub> are odd."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Orthogonality and norm",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Legendre's equation is self-adjoint: d/dx[(1−x²)y′] + n(n+1)y = 0. This is a Sturm-Liouville equation with weight 1 on [−1, 1]; see <a href=\"#sturm-liouville-problems-and-orthogonal-eigenfunctions\">3.6</a>. Solutions with different n are orthogonal."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "∫<sub>−1</sub><sup>1</sup> P<sub>m</sub>(x)P<sub>n</sub>(x) dx = 0, m ≠ n",
     "where": "m and n are nonnegative integers; the weight is 1; the interval is [−1, 1]."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "∫<sub>−1</sub><sup>1</sup> [P<sub>n</sub>(x)]² dx = 2/(2n + 1)",
     "where": "n = 0, 1, 2, …; the integral is a pure number."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Any reasonable f on [−1, 1] expands as f(x) = Σ c<sub>n</sub>P<sub>n</sub>(x), with c<sub>n</sub> = ((2n + 1)/2)∫<sub>−1</sub><sup>1</sup> f(x)P<sub>n</sub>(x) dx. Orthogonality makes least-squares approximation simple: each c<sub>n</sub> depends only on f and P<sub>n</sub>."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Some syllabi call these Legendre functions of the first kind. Here n is a nonnegative integer, so they are polynomials."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
