   "question": "Using Rodrigues' formula, find P0(x), P1(x), P2(x) and P3(x). Hence evaluate ∫<sub>−1</sub><sup>1</sup> P1(x)P3(x) dx.",
   "steps": [
    "Rodrigues' formula is P<sub>n</sub>(x) = 1/(2<sup>n</sup>n!) d<sup>n</sup>/dx<sup>n</sup>[(x²−1)<sup>n</sup>].",
    "n = 0: P<sub>0</sub>(x) = 1/(1·1) · 1 = 1.",
    "n = 1: P<sub>1</sub>(x) = 1/(2·1) · d/dx(x²−1) = (1/2)(2x) = x.",
    "n = 2: (x²−1)² = x⁴ − 2x² + 1; second derivative = 12x² − 4. P<sub>2</sub>(x) = (12x² − 4)/8 = (3x² − 1)/2.",
    "n = 3: (x²−1)³ = x⁶ − 3x⁴ + 3x² − 1; third derivative = 120x³ − 72x = 24(5x³ − 3x). P<sub>3</sub>(x) = 24(5x³ − 3x)/48 = (5x³ − 3x)/2.",
    "Multiply: P<sub>1</sub>(x)P<sub>3</sub>(x) = x·(5x³ − 3x)/2 = (5x⁴ − 3x²)/2.",
    "Integrate term by term: ∫<sub>−1</sub><sup>1</sup> x⁴ dx = 2/5, and ∫<sub>−1</sub><sup>1</sup> x² dx = 2/3.",
    "So ∫<sub>−1</sub><sup>1</sup> P1P3 dx = (1/2)[5·(2/5) − 3·(2/3)] = (1/2)[2 − 2] = 0."
   ],
   "answer": "P0 = 1, P1 = x, P2 = (3x² − 1)/2, P3 = (5x³ − 3x)/2, and ∫<sub>−1</sub><sup>1</sup> P1(x)P3(x) dx = 0.",
   "check": "from fractions import Fraction\n\ndef mul(a, b):\n    c = [Fraction(0) for _ in range(len(a) + len(b) - 1)]\n    for i, ai in enumerate(a):\n        for j, bj in enumerate(b):\n            c[i + j] += ai * bj\n    return c\n\ndef integrate_sym(a):\n    total = Fraction(0)\n    for k, ak in enumerate(a):\n        p = k + 1\n        if p % 2 == 0:\n            total += ak * Fraction(2, p)\n    return total\n\nP1 = [Fraction(0), Fraction(1)]\nP3 = [Fraction(0), Fraction(-3, 2), Fraction(0), Fraction(5, 2)]\nprod = mul(P1, P3)\nval = integrate_sym(prod)\nprint(int(val))",
